{{- /*
model.door.induce — the failure states a check can ask a subject for, as
the harness carries them.

Rendered into the harness generator's fields region. Keyed by the error
the subject reports in that state, because that is what a check names: it
asks for the state ErrMediumGone comes from, and the runner matches the
identity rather than a word somebody typed twice.

The trigger takes the instance and nothing else. It has to REACH the
subject rather than wrap it — a double answering the error in front would
leave the subject ignorant of the call, and a claim about what the
subject did before reporting the error would have nothing to be false
about.
*/ -}}
{{- define "model.door.induce" -}}
	// Induce maps an error to the trigger that puts an instance into the
	// state it is reported from. Checks that need a subject in a failure
	// state name the error, and fail naming it when this has no entry.
	//
	// The trigger acts on the instance itself: it makes the real
	// implementation start failing, rather than standing something in
	// front of it that answers without asking. A check about what your
	// implementation did before it reported the error can only be
	// answered by your implementation.
	Induce map[error]func(s T)
{{- end -}}
